<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Celts</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Celts were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> who spoke Celtic languages, spread from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. Associated with the Hallstatt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-tene-culture">La Tène cultures</a>, they sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> around 390 BCE, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in 279 BCE and settled in Galatia. Most of their lands were conquered by Rome, but Celtic languages survive in Ireland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and Brittany.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/celts.jpg" alt="Celts" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: QuartierLatin1968,The Ogre,Dbachmann · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Celts_in_Europe.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
